      "problem": "Express $S=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60$ in sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad , \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $4,$ and since $60\\div 4 = 15,$ there are $15$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\na_n =4n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=15\\mathbin{.}$ Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)=12 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{14} & =4(14)=56 \\\\ {a}_{15} & =4(15)=60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n.\n}\n$$",
